title: 'a-clock: in-app looping YouTube window + KeepAlive always-on agent'
summary: 'Resolved YouTube-as-alarm for the shipped app and made the app never-die. YouTube: inline iframes fail in a shipped webview (opaque tauri:// origin -> Error 153), and the /embed/ page errors when opened top-level too, so the app now opens the real watch page in a dedicated window via a new open_video Rust command, injecting an initialization script that keeps the HTML5 player looping (video.loop = true, reapplied each second). Scoped the hide-on-close behavior to the main window so the video window can actually close. Installed the app as a launchd KeepAlive LaunchAgent (auto-restarts on quit/crash, verified respawn in ~2s) replacing the simple login item, and cleared ~6.3 GB of safe build artifacts/caches to unblock builds.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#trigger-player'
  - '#tauri-core'
  - '#youtube'
  - '#ringing-overlay'
  - '#mobile-alarms'
files_created:
  - src/lib/mobileAlarms.ts
  - ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.ascend.a-clock.plist (local, not in repo)
files_modified:
  - src-tauri/src/lib.rs
  - src-tauri/capabilities/default.json
  - src/components/TriggerPlayer.tsx
  - src/components/RingingOverlay.tsx
