  - src/App.tsx
  - src/App.css
decisions:
  - id: youtube-watch-window-loop
    decision: Play YouTube in a dedicated window loading the watch page, with a Rust-injected script that sets video.loop=true
    rationale: Inline embeds 153 in the shipped app, and the /embed/ page also errors top-level; the real watch page plays top-level, and injecting loop on the HTML5 element restores looping the URL can't provide.
  - id: hide-on-close-main-only
    decision: Scope hide-on-close to the main window
    rationale: The global handler was intercepting the YouTube video window's close, so it could never be closed/dismissed.
  - id: keepalive-agent
    decision: Run the installed app as a launchd KeepAlive LaunchAgent instead of a login item
    rationale: User needs a local instance that never stays dead; KeepAlive auto-restarts on quit/crash (verified ~2s respawn), unlike a login item which only starts at login.
learnings:
  - YouTube /embed/ throws Error 153 both as an iframe in an opaque-origin webview AND when opened top-level; the reliable path is the real watch page loaded top-level in a dedicated webview.
  - 'Looping a YouTube video without the embed loop param: inject a script that sets the underlying <video> element''s loop=true and reapplies it on an interval.'
  - WebviewWindow initialization scripts aren't exposed in the JS API — create the window from Rust (WebviewWindowBuilder::initialization_script) to inject page scripts.
  - A global CloseRequested hide-handler must check window.label()=='main' or it traps auxiliary windows.
  - launchd KeepAlive gives a true always-on GUI app (auto-restart); the tradeoff is the tray Quit relaunches, so provide launchctl bootout as the off-switch.
